 "result": "The answer is **Emil Hansen**. Their first order, a Headset on 2026-11-05, cost \u20ac75.00, the highest first order of any customer.\n\nEach customer's first order, by date:\n\n| Customer | First order | Date | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Emil Hansen** | Headset | 2026-11-05 | **\u20ac75.00** |\n| Ben Okafor | Mouse | 2026-08-19 | \u20ac45.00 |\n| Ana Costa | Desk lamp | 2026-08-14 | \u20ac39.00 |\n| Chen Wei | USB hub | 2026-10-12 | \u20ac25.00 |\n| Dora Novak | Desk mat | 2026-07-21 | \u20ac19.00 |\n\nI picked each customer's first order by the `ordered_on` date, not the order ID. That matters for Ben Okafor: the Keyboard (\u20ac120) has the lower ID (#3), but the Mouse (#8) was ordered earlier. If you went by ID instead, Ben's first order would be the \u20ac120 Keyboard and Ben would be the answer.\n\nEmil Hansen has only placed one order, so their \"first order\" is also their only order.",
